description:
What the code does: `crate-jmap-mime/benches/mime_pipeline.rs:240-251` walks down the deepest multipart child chain using:

```rust
let mut depth = 1usize;
let mut node = &msg.part_index;
loop {
    let multipart_child = node.children.iter().find(|c| !c.children.is_empty());
    match multipart_child {
        Some(c) => {
            depth += 1;
            node = c;
        }
        None => break,
    }
}
```

What idiomatic Rust would do:

```rust
let mut depth = 1usize;
let mut node = &msg.part_index;
while let Some(c) = node.children.iter().find(|c| !c.children.is_empty()) {
    depth += 1;
    node = c;
}
```

Why it matters: the `loop { match ... { Some(...) => ..., None => break } }` shape is the canonical anti-pattern that `while let` was introduced to replace. A Rust practitioner reading the original immediately reaches for `while let` — the `loop`+`match` form signals either pre-1.0 code or someone unfamiliar with the idiom. The transformation is mechanical, shaves five lines, and produces strictly more idiomatic Rust with zero semantic change. Clippy will eventually flag this under `clippy::while_let_loop` (currently stable but only triggers on a tighter pattern; the present case slips through, which is exactly when human review matters more than the linter).

Out of scope: same file's `build_*` functions use `while s.len() < target { ... }` loops, which are correct `while` loops over a non-iterator condition — those are fine and not subject to this finding.
